routes.brief:
Plan Tate cohomology of moduli stacks of shtukas with mod p coefficients and the global cyclic base change it produces. The final theorems are Feng's Theorem 5.11 and Theorem 5.13. Qualified working comparison (Theorem 5.11 with finite étale π:X′→X): for X a smooth projective curve over F_ell with ell not p, H reductive over its function field F with a parahoric model, E/F cyclic of degree p and G = Res_{X'/X}(H_{X'}), fix a closed point x_0 and a closed finite subscheme D; then for j in {0,1}, under the isomorphism T^j(Sht_{G,D+infinity x_0, empty};1) = H_c^0(Sht_{H,D+infinity x_0,empty};1) induced by the identification of Sht_H with the sigma-fixed locus of Sht_G, the excursion operator S_{I,Nm^{(1/p)}(V),Nm^{(1/p)}(x),Nm^{(1/p)}(xi), (gamma_i)} of Exc(Gamma,^LG)^sigma acts as S_{I,Res_BC(V),x,xi,(gamma_i)} of Exc(Gamma,^LH), and the operators S_{I,N.V,(N.x) composed with Delta_p, nabla_p composed with (N.xi),(gamma_i)} act as zero. Qualified working comparison (Theorem 5.13 with finite étale π): an L-parameter arising from the action of Exc(Gamma,^LH) on H^0_c(Sht_H;Sat(1))_alpha has its image under phi_BC arising from the action of Exc(Gamma,^LG) on H^0_c(Sht_G;Sat(1))_{phi(alpha)}; equivalently, if rho into ^LH(k) is automorphic then phi_BC composed with rho is automorphic, which is strong base change, not merely agreement of unramified Hecke eigensystems. Cover: the base change setup for shtukas and the finite-étale identification of the shtuka stack for H with the sigma-fixed locus of the one for G on representable truncations; Tate cohomology of moduli of shtukas and of the colimit over Harder-Narasimhan bounds and level at a fixed point, at infinite level; Drinfeld's lemma for Tate cohomology, that is the factorisation of the partial Weil group action through the product of copies of the Weil group, together with the Eichler-Shimura relation of Xue and its Tate cohomology form and the finite-type filtration it needs; the axiomatics of admissible families of functors and the excursion action they carry; the Tate diagonal homomorphism of a commutative algebra in characteristic p, the unique extension of characters it gives, the norm and norm-element formulas in the excursion algebra and the subalgebra Exc' they generate; the Frobenius twist and linearization of algebras; and the functoriality of excursion algebras along an admissible L-homomorphism together with the base change homomorphism phi_BC and the comparison between the algebraic and geometric L-groups that relates the two normalisations. Import from: Global shtukas and Langlands over function fields (GlobalShtukasAndFunctionFieldLanglands) for shtuka stacks with legs, level and truncations, the Satake functor on them, partial Frobenius and Drinfeld's lemma for ordinary cohomology, and the excursion algebra with its reconstruction of semisimple parameters, all of which the parent roadmap plans with ell-adic characteristic-zero coefficients; this Part II is the mod p theory, where finiteness of the cuspidal sector is replaced by Xue's extension to all compactly supported functions. Import also Sheaf-theoretic Smith theory and the base change functor for the Satake category (SheafTheoreticSmithTheory) for the Smith operation, equivariant localisation and the functor BC, and Reductive groups, Part II (ReductiveGroupsPartII) for the dual group and the L-group.

Corrections from the review (REV-PAPER-FENG-24):
- Scope. With k = F̄_p coefficients and Γ = Gal(F^s/F), this roadmap also owns the following:
  - the geometric L-group ^LG^geom, built from the scheme-theoretic Gr_{G,F^s} (item 8);
  - the excursion algebra in both presentations, with their comparison (items 11 and 13);
  - the reconstruction of semisimple L-parameters from its characters, in the modular form of Böckle–Harris–Khare–Thorne, Theorem 4.5 (item 12);
  - homomorphisms of L-groups over Γ (item 14);
  - the Satake functor with k-coefficients on the Hecke stack and on shtukas (item 53);
  - Lemma B.5, Drinfeld's lemma for modules of finite type over a finitely generated algebra (item 104).
- Imports:
  - for local Weil groups, LanglandsParameterStacks:LP2:excursion-presentation and LP2:semisimple-characters;
  - for the local form of Proposition 2.1, GeometricSatakeAndFusion:GS4:integral-dual-group;
  - the Hecke stack and the map from shtukas from GlobalShtukasAndFunctionFieldLanglands:GS.1–GS.2, and Drinfeld's lemma from GS.4;
  - the L-group ^LG^alg of a group over a global function field from ReductiveGroupsPartII:RG2.5, whose construction does not depend on the base field (say so);
  - the Tate diagonal (Definition 5.14, Lemma 5.15) and the Frobenius twist of algebras (Construction 5.17) from SheafTheoreticSmithTheory, where they have moved.
- Mistakes in the paper to plan around (the source issues give the details):
  - Lemma 2.7: φ^* sends ρ to the semisimplification (φ∘ρ)^ss, not to φ∘ρ. In characteristic p, φ∘ρ need not be semisimple (Sym^p on SL_2(F_p)). Theorems 1.2, 1.4 and 1.6 pass from equal excursion characters to isomorphic parameters, and for that they need the lemma that φ_BC∘ρ is semisimple whenever ρ is. Plan it (new item).
  - Proposition 2.4, (2.3): the printed indices are wrong. The correct formula is ν(S_{{0,…,n},f,(γ_0,…,γ_n)}) = f(ρ_ν(γ_0),…,ρ_ν(γ_n)).
  - (2.1): the isomorphism ^LG^alg ≅ ^LG^geom needs a lift χ̃ whose values are fixed by act^alg, for example 2ρ̂∘ε. An arbitrary lift fails for unitary groups.
  - Theorem 5.13: the proof gives the support statement for all of H^0_c(Sht_G;1), not for its φ_*(α)-component. For finite étale π, run the argument on the σ-stable φ_*(α)-sector using qualified item 60. For ramified π, the previous E41 component repair needs the new descent/summand input (E58).
  - Appendix B: Lemma B.4 gives stability of 𝔐^μ only under nonnegative powers of partial Frobenius. The inverses come from the relation of Lemma B.3, whose constant term is the invertible Hecke operator of det W. The 𝔐^μ need not increase with μ, but the conclusion does not need them to.

Corrected design contracts (FIX-RT-PAPER-FENG-24):
- The whole-fixed-locus identification and its localization comparisons (items 60,62,63,64) assume finite étale π. Its proof uses X′×_X X′=C_p×X′ and effective fppf descent. Retain unrestricted ramified Theorems 1.6/5.13 as source targets requiring a separate ramified descent/localization construction. Build that input in this same Part II: record inertia actions at each branch place, isolate effective fppf descent, and prove the comparison/summand stable under geometric creation, annihilation, Γ-transport, partial Frobenius and the Exc′-action. If a branch-level construction is used, specify sufficient full level at every branch place and prove descent, change-of-level compatibility and the claimed level control. This brief does not assert a sufficient branch-level theorem. Smith localization uses the whole fixed scheme; a smaller descended subfunctor is not interchangeable with it. Test the F_7 framed t=u³ counterexample at all n, and reprove E41 on the β-sector before claiming its ramified support statement. The main existence target is not disproved by this counterexample.
- Item 56 uses additive k-linear geometric functors F_I:Rep_k(Ĝ^I)→Mod_A for a central k-algebra A, with natural coherent χ, normalized unit transport, the a_γ cocycle and explicit trivial transport in unused geometric legs. Full-L-group W supplies r_γ, so the middle action is F_I(r_γ)a_γ. Creation and annihilation are applied to geometric maps, with only diagonal-Ĝ invariance. Prove all six item-13 relations by the arguments in the fixes report, including unit, zero, scalar, sum, external product, duality and inflation; no strong monoidality of F_I is required. Verify the actual Satake/cohomology and Tate instances, not merely fusion. Test the quadratic Γ-character, where S=χ(γ) id even though creation is not an L-group morphism. Items 53, rev-20 and rev-23 use these same geometric operations before descent.
- Route 3 imports this qualified comparison and the ramified repair; it does not duplicate descent or assume deep auxiliary level repairs ramification.
